              "k": "Translation / replication",
              "v": "Gamarnik & Andino · Genes & Development 12, 2293–2304 (1998)",
              "g": "Supports distinguishing genome translation from RNA replication and a transition between those uses. Studied a specific non-enveloped virus; does not validate the envelope, entry route or universal timing in this composite.",
              "link": "https://genesdev.cshlp.org/content/12/15/2293.full"
            },
            {
              "k": "RNA synthesis",
              "v": "Wang et al. · Cell 182, 417–428.e13 (2020)",
              "g": "Structural work supports a polymerase engaging RNA during synthesis. This cartoon omits sequence, polarity detail, cofactors and measured kinetics; it does not depict the named virus studied in that paper.",
              "link": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0092867420306292"
            },
            {
              "k": "Accessible primary text",
              "v": "Wang et al. · full article archive",
              "g": "Accessible primary-source copy used to verify the polymerase claim. The hypothetical interruption here is not the intervention tested by that study.",
              "link": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7242921/"
            },
            {
              "k": "Authored hypothesis",
              "v": "Interrupt further synthesis → stop additional genome supply",
              "g": "One completed new RNA exists before the fork. The alternate assumes that copy can be packaged and released with existing proteins; the cited studies do not establish this generic output. Real replication–assembly coupling varies. Counts are neither yield nor efficacy estimates."
            },
            {
              "k": "Abstraction",
              "v": "One cell · symbolic genome supply · one branch point",
              "g": "The pathway bridge names the complementary intermediate but compresses its copying rounds. The drawing omits molecular detail, compartment organization, competing processes, host responses and real timescales. Entry, packaging and release routes differ across viruses."
            }
          ]
        },
        "bullets": [
          "Rank 1 · Copy-gate comparison (chosen): keeps both paths identical until one defined dependency changes; strongest for separating translation from genome copying.",
          "Rank 2 · Entry-gate comparison: easier to show at the membrane, but removes the shared intracellular history and teaches a different dependency.",
          "Rank 3 · Assembly-gate comparison: makes residual RNA visible, but requires more structural detail and shifts attention away from RNA synthesis."
        ]
      }
